## Phase 1 - Introduce session settings identities

☐ Add a dedicated in-memory window-settings repository keyed by `settings_id`.
☐ Define the session settings snapshot: ordered filters, unit scope, result
  sort key/reverse/phase, stats sort key/reverse/phase, and window frame.
☐ Normalize `settings_id`: accept non-empty strings and map missing or empty
  identities to the stable `default` identity; reject other value types.
☐ Make repository `load()` and field-level `update()` operations defensively
  copy nested filters, sort records, and frame data so callers cannot alias saved
  or live window state.
☐ Make updates merge only the named fields into the latest identity snapshot;
  never replace unrelated fields from a window's potentially stale local copy.
☐ Add `clear()` for lifecycle handling and isolated tests.
☐ Keep the repository module-local and session-only; do not use JSON,
  `script-manager.getModStatePath()`, or any other filesystem persistence.
☐ Remove the single saved-filter snapshot from `filter_state.lua`; retain only
  construction, validation, projections, and mutation of supplied filter state.
☐ Add repository tests for identity normalization, missing identities,
  same-identity reopen, distinct identities, defensive copying, field-level
  merging, stale-window updates, overwrites, and clearing.
☐ Add the settings repository to `module_registry.lua` in dependency order and
  update `module_registry_test.lua`, `tests/run.lua`, and
  `support/soulsearch_env.lua` for the new runtime module and suite.

Phase gate:
☐ Multiple settings identities can coexist without sharing mutable state.
☐ `filter_state` tests remain green after persistence ownership is removed.

## Phase 2 - Make window construction instance-driven

☐ Define the optional `ui.open(options)` window specification with
  `settings_id`, `filters`, `unit_scope`, `result_sort`, `stats_sort`, and
  `frame` fields.
☐ Resolve initial settings in this order: explicit option override, saved
  snapshot for the identity, then existing defaults.
☐ Validate filter and unit-scope overrides through the existing domain modules
  instead of trusting caller-provided values.
☐ Normalize sort records to supported keys, boolean reverse values, and valid
  phases; fall back to the existing unsorted defaults for malformed values.
☐ Normalize frames to the current minimum dimensions and clamp positions and
  extents to the available screen before constructing the window.
☐ Copy all option data before storing it on a window.
☐ Pass the resolved identity and settings into `SoulSearchScreen` and
  `SoulSearchWindow` constructors.
☐ Move module-level saved sort and frame values into the window-owned settings
  snapshot/repository path.
☐ Load the unit scope from the resolved settings instead of always resetting to
  `fort_residents`.
☐ Keep query strings, results, selected unit, picker queries, modal visibility,
  tooltip state, and refresh suppression flags instance-only and non-persistent.
☐ Centralize session-setting updates behind window methods that submit only the
  fields changed by that action.
☐ Update only `filters`, `unit_scope`, `result_sort`, or `stats_sort` after the
  corresponding action; update only `frame` after a user drag/resize or when an
  explicit frame override must establish the identity's initial geometry.
☐ Ensure opening with explicit scoped overrides establishes the resolved
  identity settings on first change/dismissal without replacing unspecified saved
  fields.

Phase gate:
☐ Two directly constructed windows can use different settings and transient
  state without affecting each other.
☐ A later window inherits the latest snapshot for its identity.

## Phase 3 - Replace the singleton with a live-screen registry

☐ Replace the module-level `view` singleton with a registry of live
  `SoulSearchScreen` instances.
☐ Make every successful `ui.open(options)` create, show, register, and return a
  new screen instead of raising an existing one.
☐ Give each screen exactly one SoulSearch window and its tooltip overlay.
☐ Add transient cascade placement for new windows whose resolved frame overlaps
  a live SoulSearch window: offset diagonally, wrap/clamp to the screen, and let an
  explicit `frame` override take precedence.
☐ Track frame persistence separately from placement: do not write an automatic
  cascade offset back unless the user subsequently moves/resizes the window; do
  persist an explicit frame override for the identity.
☐ Make registry cleanup idempotent and invoke it from both `onDismiss()` and
  `onDestroy()` so externally removed screens cannot leave stale entries.
☐ During dismissal/destruction, save a dirty or explicit frame at most once and
  unregister only that screen; an untouched auto-cascaded frame remains transient.
☐ Ensure closing one window cannot clear, dismiss, or mutate another window.
☐ Keep unavailable-world validation ahead of screen construction and return
  `nil` when opening is rejected.
☐ Ensure the public `soulsearch` command and configurable Ctrl+F keybinding now
  spawn additional default-identity windows.
☐ Extract the live-screen registry into a small pure-Lua module so it can be
  tested with dummy screen handles without emulating `gui.ZScreen`,
  `widgets.Window`, or `defclass`.
☐ Add pure-Lua registry tests for distinct screen handles, counts, overlap
  lookup/cascade inputs, idempotent removal, and out-of-order dismissal; verify
  actual repeated `ui.open()` construction and returned screens in live DFHack.
☐ Add the registry module/suite to `module_registry.lua`,
  `module_registry_test.lua`, `tests/run.lua`, and `support/soulsearch_env.lua`.

Phase gate:
☐ At least three SoulSearch windows can coexist, and closing them in any order
  leaves the remaining windows functional.

## Phase 4 - Make reload and lifecycle handling multi-window safe

☐ Add a registry operation that safely snapshots/iterates all live screens for
  bulk dismissal or invalidation.
☐ In `soulsearch.lua`, obtain the old loaded UI module and call
  `old_ui.dismiss_all()` before `devel/clear-script-env` clears any consumer
  modules, so no window retains callbacks into obsolete classes.
☐ Consume the `reload` control argument in the command layer; after rebuilding,
  call the new `ui.open()` with no literal `"reload"` argument.
☐ Update `soulsearch_command_test.lua` to assert teardown occurs before the
  clear command and that exactly one new default window opens afterward.
☐ Ensure dismissal callbacks can modify the registry during bulk teardown
  without skipping screens or raising iteration errors.
☐ Decide lifecycle ownership explicitly: retain settings across ordinary
  close/reopen and world changes while modules remain loaded; clear settings only
  when the settings module is reloaded or its test-only `clear()` is invoked.
☐ Preserve the existing cache reset behavior in `lifecycle.prepare_for_world()`.
☐ Add command/registry tests for reload with zero, one, and multiple live
  screens, including idempotent callbacks and dismissal during registry iteration.
☐ Verify reload leaves no stale registry entries or callable old-window
  callbacks.

Phase gate:
☐ Development reload dismisses every old screen before clearing modules, resets
  module-owned session settings, rebuilds cleanly, and opens exactly one new
  default window.

## Phase 5 - Scoped-window readiness and compatibility coverage

☐ Add tests showing explicit filter and unit-scope overrides initialize only
  the new window and do not mutate repository inputs or existing windows.
☐ Verify explicit overrides replace only the fields supplied by the caller;
  unspecified fields continue from that identity's saved snapshot or defaults.
☐ Add tests showing windows with the same identity are independent while open;
  field updates merge into the latest saved identity, and subsequently opened
  windows inherit the merged state without stale-field rollback.
☐ Add tests showing different settings identities retain separate filters,
  scope, sorting, and geometry for the session.
☐ Verify malformed or stale scoped filter overrides use normal filter-state
  validation and restore the required positive default race scope.
☐ Document `ui.open(options)` beside the future scoped-window work, including
  the stable `settings_id` contract and session-only lifetime.
☐ Document that callers must pass initial scope through options rather than
  mutating a live window or module global after creation.
☐ Keep preset JSON persistence independent from window settings identities.
☐ Update README development-reload guidance: users no longer need to close
  SoulSearch manually before `soulsearch reload`.
☐ Update README usage text to say repeated command or Ctrl+F invocations create
  additional windows and only one ZScreen has keyboard focus at a time.

Phase gate:
☐ A future creatures-menu or profession integration can create a scoped window
  through `ui.open{settings_id=..., filters=..., unit_scope=...}` without changing
  the multi-window lifecycle or settings architecture.

## Phase 6 - Automated and live validation

☐ Run `tools/Build.ps1` and confirm all production Lua files compile.
☐ Run `tools/Test.ps1` and confirm the full suite passes.
☐ Run `tools/Publish.ps1` and `tools/VerifyPackage.ps1`; this refactor adds or
  changes runtime modules and the module registry.
☐ Run `git diff --check` and inspect the final diff for unrelated changes,
  accidental filesystem-backed settings, stale singleton fields, and missing
  module/test registration.
☐ In a loaded fortress, open at least three windows through both the command
  and Ctrl+F.
☐ Give each window different filters, unit scope, query, selected result, sort
  modes, position, and size; confirm no live cross-window updates occur.
☐ Confirm newly spawned windows are visibly cascaded and clamped on screen
  instead of appearing perfectly stacked, while explicit frames are respected.
☐ Exercise filter and preset modals independently in multiple windows.
☐ Close the windows in a different order from creation and confirm the others
  remain visible, interactive, and correctly focused.
☐ Reopen the `default` identity and confirm the latest session filters, unit
  scope, sorting, and frame are restored.
☐ Open two distinct test identities and confirm each restores only its own
  session settings.
☐ Open two windows with the same identity, make interleaved changes to different
  fields, close them in reverse order, and confirm no newer field is rolled back.
☐ Exercise result navigation, stats tooltips, filter hitboxes, zoom, drag, and
  resize to confirm existing UI behavior is preserved.
☐ Run `soulsearch reload` with multiple windows open and confirm all old windows
  close cleanly and a new window opens with no stale callbacks.
☐ Confirm no window-settings file is created anywhere in mod-state or the mod
  payload.

Final gate:
☐ Multiple independent SoulSearch windows are supported.
☐ Session settings identities are ready for future scoped callers.
☐ Existing search, UI, preset, packaging, and lifecycle behavior remains green.
☐ Mark the corresponding item in `docs/project.todo` complete only after the
  automated gates and fortress-mode multi-window smoke pass succeed.

## Explicitly deferred

- Durable settings persistence across DFHack restarts.
- File formats, schema versions, or migrations for window settings.
- Base-game unit-card stats popovers.
- Creatures-menu widgets and concrete scoped-window integrations.
- Live synchronization between already-open windows sharing a settings ID.
- Resizable panel layout work beyond preserving current window behavior.
